# Chapter Seven — The Other Chair

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.2. This chapter opens the first private investigation paths. The working crime engine remains unratified. No interaction branches yet.

## Scene 15 — The call before dinner

= Present: Henry and Nell.

= Immediate pressure: Nell wants Henry to begin without allowing her last willingness to hear Paul to become forgiveness, reconciliation, or motive.

INT. CALDER INVESTIGATIONS - MORNING

At ten to seven that morning, Nell Avery telephoned Henry's office from Marian's house and asked whether he took clients he had eaten dinner with. He said nine thirty. At eight, Henry telephoned Ruth, told her Nell was coming to retain him, and asked for Paul's current address. Ruth said she would bring it at ten.

At nine twenty, Henry turns the card in the frosted-glass door from CLOSED to OPEN.

Nell Avery is already standing on the other side.

HENRY
How long have you been there?

NELL
Not long.

HENRY
I said nine thirty.

NELL
I know.

HENRY
You could have knocked.

NELL
You were opening.

He opens the door.

Daylight makes the office less forgiving. The two client chairs do not match. The filing cabinet lists slightly toward the wall. Henry has washed the two cups from yesterday and left them upside down on a towel.

Nell takes the chair that does not face the window.

Henry sets a legal pad on his desk and remains standing.

HENRY
We should agree on the job before I begin it.

NELL
That is why I came.

HENRY
Ward investigates Paul's death. I find out what Paul brought for you, whether it belonged to June, how he obtained it, and where any of it went.

NELL
And what he meant to tell me.

HENRY
If I can.

NELL
You left that part out.

HENRY
It is the part he may have taken with him.

Nell opens her handbag. She places her checkbook on the desk, writes without asking his rate, tears out the check, and turns it facedown.

NELL
If you cannot find it, I am still paying for the attempt.

HENRY
Anything physical that may concern his death goes to Ward. I will tell you that I found it. I may not be able to show it to you.

NELL
All right.

HENRY
If the two questions become the same question, Ward gets there first.

NELL
He can have it. I want the other one.

HENRY
Ruth is my friend.

NELL
I know. I saw you come in together.

HENRY
That may matter.

NELL
Then tell me when it matters.

HENRY
And if I find something you don't want to hear—

NELL
You tell me badly.

Henry turns the check over, folds it once, and puts it beneath the legal pad.

HENRY
Does Lydia know you retained me?

NELL
She called Marian's house at seven. I told her.

HENRY
What did she say?

NELL
That I could come to her apartment after one.

HENRY
You could.

NELL
She said you could not. I said I would not come without you.

HENRY
And?

NELL
One o'clock. Neither of us touches anything until she shows us.

Henry writes Lydia's address near the top of the pad.

HENRY
What would you recognize?

NELL
Of my mother's?

HENRY
Yes.

NELL
Her handwriting. Her voice. The things we kept in the apartment.

HENRY
What things?

NELL
I don't know. Boxes. Letters. Clothes I was too young for and then too old for. Marian kept the photographs. Lydia kept whatever she thought the rest of us would lose.

HENRY
What did Paul take when he left?

NELL
His clothes. Two boxes of papers. The good frying pan.

HENRY
Were the papers his?

NELL
He said they were.

HENRY
Did you look?

NELL
No.

She rubs at a place on one glove where no mark is visible.

NELL
It seemed important that I not look. We were trying to leave each other decently.

HENRY
Did you?

NELL
No.

Henry writes *two boxes*.

HENRY
Last night Paul put a letter on the table. What did you expect him to say?

NELL
That he was sorry. He had said that part twice.

Henry stops writing.

HENRY
Twice?

Nell looks at the check beneath his pad.

NELL
Once on the telephone.

Henry sits.

HENRY
When?

NELL
Before dinner. Not long before I left home.

HENRY
Paul called you.

NELL
Yes. I nearly didn't pick it up. I was doing my face.

HENRY
What did he say?

NELL
He said Lydia had found him. He said she wanted him at Tolland's and asked if I was coming.

HENRY
You had not answered Lydia.

NELL
Not then.

HENRY
What else?

NELL
He said he had something that should have reached me years ago.

HENRY
Did he say what?

NELL
No. I asked. He said he wanted to show me, not name it over a telephone.

HENRY
Then?

NELL
He asked if I would listen after dinner.

HENRY
You agreed.

NELL
I said he would speak first. No Lydia. No Ruth. If he stopped halfway and waited for somebody to make the rest bearable, I would leave.

HENRY
What did he say?

NELL
All right.

HENRY
Anything after that?

NELL
I hung up.

The fan turns toward them. A corner of the check lifts, then falls.

HENRY
Does Ward know about the call?

NELL
No.

HENRY
Did he ask?

NELL
Yes.

HENRY
What did you tell him?

NELL
That Paul had not called me.

Henry draws one line beneath *two boxes*. He does not write the new fact yet.

NELL
I had just sat for an hour above his body with Marian's coat on my chair and a policeman writing down what I had been drinking, and then a stranger in a brown suit asked me whether I had arranged to be alone with my husband after dinner, and I knew how it sounded, and I said no.

HENRY
That is one reason.

NELL
I did not want them to make my yes into forgiveness.

HENRY
That is another.

NELL
He had been dead three hours and everyone had already begun speaking about what he meant to do. As if intending to tell the truth at the end made the years before it honest.

HENRY
And the first reason?

Nell looks at him.

NELL
I was afraid they would think I killed him.

Henry writes *call before supper*.

HENRY
Ward needs to hear it from you.

NELL
I hired you to find my mother's property.

HENRY
You did.

NELL
Not to repair my statement.

HENRY
This is not a repair.

She takes off the glove she has been rubbing and lays it across the other.

NELL
Will you call him now?

HENRY
Now.

Henry pulls the telephone toward him. He dials police headquarters and asks for Detective Ward. A desk officer transfers the call.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Calder.

HENRY
Nell Avery retained me to identify property of June Avery's that Paul may have possessed. Not to investigate his death.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Go on.

HENRY
Miss Avery needs to correct her statement. Paul telephoned her before supper. They arranged to speak privately afterward.

There is a silence at the other end.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Put her on.

Henry gives Nell the receiver.

NELL
Detective Ward. I told you he had not called. That was not true.

She listens.

NELL
Before I left home.

She listens.

NELL
Yes. Alone.

She listens longer.

NELL
I understood the question last night.

Another silence.

NELL
I was afraid.

Ward speaks. Nell closes her eyes, opens them, and answers the rest without looking at Henry.

NELL
I will come in this afternoon.

She returns the receiver to Henry.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
I will take the full correction here.

HENRY
She will be there.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Do not use that call to question another Tolland's witness.

HENRY
I won't.

WARD (TELEPHONE)
Everything we retained from Tolland's stays with us. If you locate Avery property somewhere else, do not move it. Call me.

HENRY
Understood.

Ward hangs up. Henry replaces the receiver.

Someone knocks directly beneath Henry's painted name.

Nell looks at the clock. It is ten.

## Scene 16 — The other chair

= Present: Henry, Nell, and Ruth.

= Immediate pressure: Ruth has come to give Henry an address. Nell needs to know how often Ruth has made Paul's departures possible.

INT. CALDER INVESTIGATIONS - MORNING

Henry opens the door. Ruth stands outside with a small envelope in one hand.

RUTH
You asked for an address.

She sees Nell.

HENRY
We ran long.

RUTH
I can come back.

NELL
No.

Ruth looks at Henry.

HENRY
I can finish with Nell first.

RUTH
No.

There is no third client chair. Henry brings the straight chair from behind his desk. Ruth starts toward the chair she used yesterday, sees Nell's gloves on its arm, and takes the straight chair facing the window.

She places the envelope on Henry's desk.

RUTH
Paul rented a room at the Kenton Rooms on South Alvarado. The room number is inside.

HENRY
Have you been there?

RUTH
Never. He came to me.

NELL
You knew where he was.

RUTH
I knew where he slept.

NELL
What is the difference?

RUTH
With Paul? Sometimes a week.

The answer reaches both women at the same time. Neither smiles.

HENRY
When did he give you the address?

RUTH
In May. He came to my office.

NELL
Lydia had been trying to find him.

RUTH
Lydia did not ask me.

NELL
Would you have told her?

RUTH
No.

HENRY
Why not?

RUTH
Because Paul did not want Lydia to know.

NELL
That has always been enough for you.

Ruth touches the envelope but does not take it back.

RUTH
There is something else.

She opens her handbag and takes out a small brass room key on a plain ring.

RUTH
Paul gave it to me in May. I put it in the back of my desk drawer and forgot it. I found it when I went looking for the address. He had locked himself out twice in one month and said his landlady was tired of coming downstairs.

HENRY
Have you used it?

RUTH
No. It was under a year of receipts.

HENRY
Does Ward know?

RUTH
I called his office when I found it. They want it at eleven.

HENRY
Have they been to the room?

RUTH
A detective and a photographer went through it this morning. Ward's office says they have released it to Mrs. Vale. She will let us in after one. She has the master key.

HENRY
You spoke to her.

RUTH
This morning. She will stay while we are inside.

Ruth puts the key into the envelope.

NELL
If he took something from the apartment when he left me, I am the person who can identify it. Not Lydia. Not you. Not whatever Paul wrote in that letter.

RUTH
Then you should be there.

NELL
So should you.

RUTH
Why?

NELL
Because I know what belonged to my mother. You know what your brother could make look like his.

Ruth looks down at the key.

NELL
When he left me, did he go straight to you?

RUTH
I thought you knew that.

NELL
I knew he stayed with you. I do not know what you did.

RUTH
He had already packed.

NELL
That was not the question.

RUTH
He slept on my couch for a few nights.

NELL
How many?

RUTH
Five.

She considers it.

RUTH
Six. He arrived after midnight. I did not count that one.

HENRY
Then where did he go?

RUTH
I found him a room. I gave him the deposit.

NELL
You paid him to leave.

RUTH
No. He had left. I paid the deposit because I wanted my couch back.

NELL
Was it a loan?

RUTH
We called it one.

NELL
Did he repay you?

RUTH
No.

Nell nods as if one old detail has finally found the correct place.

NELL
Last night you said you would not explain him for me. This time.

RUTH
Yes.

NELL
What did you do the first time?

Ruth turns toward the window. The glass reflects the room more clearly than it shows the building opposite.

RUTH
Paul sat at my kitchen table with the telephone in front of him. He kept asking what he should say to you.

NELL
And you told him.

RUTH
I said he should not pretend the marriage had been good until the moment he walked out.

NELL
What words did you give him?

RUTH
Nell—

NELL
I remember the words. I want to hear whether you do.

Ruth looks at her.

RUTH
“We had already left each other. I was only the first to go.”

The office is quiet.

NELL
He never spoke like that.

RUTH
No.

NELL
How much of it was yours?

RUTH
All of it.

NELL
He said it to me as if he had discovered it.

RUTH
I thought it was true.

NELL
The marriage was ending. That does not mean we had agreed on how he would leave.

Ruth begins to answer. Nell does not raise her voice.

NELL
You made leaving sound like something we had decided together.

Ruth stops.

Henry moves the envelope so the fan will not lift it. He asks no question.

RUTH
He was my brother. He had been in my apartment for six days. He would not call you until he knew how the call would end.

NELL
He would not call.

RUTH
No.

NELL
And you wanted him to.

RUTH
I wanted him to make the call. Then I wanted the call to be over.

Nell puts on one glove. The second does not go on properly. She removes it and starts again.

RUTH
I am sorry.

NELL
I know.

Ruth looks from Nell to Henry.

RUTH
What happened before I came in?

Henry looks at Nell.

NELL
Paul called me before supper. I told Ward he had not. I corrected it before you came.

Ruth turns back to Nell.

RUTH
He had already asked you to listen?

NELL
Yes.

RUTH
Then why did he ask me downstairs?

NELL
I don't know. He always asked you.

RUTH
Not always.

NELL
Last night.

RUTH
Yes.

Nell finishes putting on the second glove.

## Scene 17 — Permission

= Present: Henry, Nell, and Ruth.

= Immediate pressure: Each possible place belongs to somebody else. Henry needs access that does not pretend to be authority.

INT. CALDER INVESTIGATIONS - MORNING

No one speaks for a while.

Ruth puts the envelope containing Paul's key into her handbag.

NELL
Did he like the room?

RUTH
He said the water came out brown.

NELL
He always complained about hot water.

RUTH
It was the cold water.

NELL
Then he was right.

Ruth looks at her. Nell looks down at her gloves.

HENRY
We can enter Lydia's apartment because she agreed. We can enter Paul's room because the police released it and Mrs. Vale agreed to open the door. We do not remove anything from either place.

NELL
If I recognize something of June's?

HENRY
You tell me. We leave it where it is and call Ward.

NELL
Then we leave it.

HENRY
I do not give either of you what the other tells me in confidence. Ruth, you are free not to help.

RUTH
Paul was my brother. I will have to sort his things whether I help you or not.

She looks at Nell.

RUTH
You should not have to do it alone.

NELL
I want to be in the room.

RUTH
I told Mrs. Vale you would be.

NELL
Before you asked me.

RUTH
Henry said you hired him to find your mother's property. I thought you would want to see the room yourself.

NELL
You could have asked.

RUTH
I could have.

NELL
All right.

Nell gathers her handbag.

NELL
I will see you both after one.

She leaves first.

Ruth moves toward the chair Nell has vacated. She rests her hand on its back but does not sit.

HENRY
Coffee?

RUTH
Yes.

Henry turns over one of the clean cups and pours.

RUTH
No. I'm sorry. I thought I wanted it.

Henry drinks it himself.

RUTH
You knew I had helped him leave.

HENRY
I knew you helped. I did not know what you said for him.

RUTH
Is it worse now?

HENRY
It hurt her.

RUTH
I know.

HENRY
Then yes.

Ruth takes the envelope from her handbag.

RUTH
I am going to Ward.

HENRY
I know.

She leaves.

Henry returns to the legal pad. Beneath *call before supper*, he writes three questions:

1. What did Paul bring back?
2. What did Lydia keep?
3. Where else did June's sentence go?

Beneath the questions are two addresses: Paul's rented room and Lydia's packed apartment.

Henry does not number them.
